# BCQuality entry-point contract

The authoritative entry-point skill is vendored at `<plugin>/content/bcquality/entry.md` (sourced from `microsoft/BCQuality/skills/entry.md` at the SHA pinned in `content/PINNED.json`). **Read that file in full when you need to route a task.** This rule is just a pointer.

## What Entry defines

- **Task context input**: `goal`, `inputs-available[]`, optional dimension filters (`technologies`, `bc-version`, `countries`, `application-area`), `enabled-layers`, `disabled-skills`.
- **Candidate set**: all action skills under `*/skills/**/*.md` across enabled layers (meta-skills in `/skills/` are excluded — Entry never dispatches Entry).
- **Relevance**: candidates pass if their `kind` is `action-skill`, their declared `inputs` intersect with `inputs-available`, and their dimension filters match.
- **Worklist (the 3-tier ranking)**:
  1. Goal match — score `description` and `id` against the task goal. Prefer exact keyword overlap before fuzzy signals.
  2. Super-skill precedence — when goal broadly matches a super-skill, dispatch the super-skill and drop the listed sub-skills (`superseded-by-super-skill`). When goal specifically names a concern a sub-skill handles, dispatch the sub-skill and drop the super-skill (`narrower-sub-skill-selected`).
  3. Layer precedence — when two skills share the same `id` across layers, keep the highest-precedence one (`/custom/` > `/community/` > `/microsoft/`).
- **Dispatch record output**: `{ outcome: "routed" | "no-match" | "failed", dispatch[], skipped[] }`. Each `dispatch[]` entry names a skill, its rationale, and the strict intersection of inputs to pass.

## Skill discovery in this plugin

Action skills live in:

- `<plugin>/content/bcquality/microsoft/skills/review/` — `al-code-review.md` (super-skill) + 6 leaves.
- `<plugin>/content/bcquality/community/skills/` — empty in upstream today.
- `<plugin>/content/bcquality/custom/skills/` — empty by default. Add local action skills here.

## How the plugin's commands consume Entry

- `/bcq-review` invokes the `al-entry` Cursor skill, which reads `entry.md`, builds the dispatch record, and invokes each named skill in turn.
- `/bcq-review-performance`, `/bcq-review-security`, etc. bypass Entry and dispatch the corresponding leaf skill directly with goal-specific dispatch.
- `/al-entry` (the Cursor skill name) NEVER dispatches Entry recursively.

## When to load this rule

- Before invoking `al-entry` to route a task.
- When debugging why a particular leaf review skill was or was not dispatched.
- Before adding a custom action skill (to understand discovery semantics).
